llm_answer:
To convert images to PDF and Word documents in a UWP (Universal Windows Platform) application using C#, you have to use libraries that are compatible with UWP since traditional libraries like iTextSharp do not support this platform. Below, I’ll detail what libraries you can use for this purpose and provide some code snippets to give you a head start.

### Libraries to Use

1. **PDF Generation**: For generating PDFs in a UWP app, you can use:
   - **PDFSharp**: A well-known library for creating and processing PDFs.
   - **Windows.Data.Pdf**: The built-in UWP namespace, but primarily for reading PDFs.
   - **Syncfusion PDF Library**: Available in the Syncfusion UWP UI controls which support PDF creation.
   - **DinkToPdf**: A lightweight .NET library for generating PDFs.

2. **Word Document Generation**: For creating Word documents, you can use:
   - **Open XML SDK**: Allows you to create and manipulate Word documents. This SDK is compatible with UWP.
   - **DocX**: Another library that provides an easier way to create and manipulate Word documents.

### PDF Conversion Example

Here’s an example of how you might use the **Syncfusion PDF Library** to convert an image to a PDF.

1. **Install Syncfusion**: You can install the Syncfusion PDF library via NuGet in your UWP project.

   ```
   Install-Package Syncfusion.Pdf.WinUI
   ```

2. **Code to Convert Image to PDF**:

   ```csharp
   using Syncfusion.Pdf;
   using Syncfusion.Pdf.Graphics;
   using Windows.Storage;
   using Windows.Storage.Pickers;
   using Windows.Storage.Streams;
   using System.IO;

   private async void ConvertImageToPdf()
   {
       // Select an image file
       var picker = new FileOpenPicker();
       picker.ViewMode = PickerViewMode.Thumbnail;
       picker.SuggestedStartLocation = PickerLocationId.PicturesLibrary;
       picker.FileTypeFilter.Add(".jpg");
       picker.FileTypeFilter.Add(".png");
       StorageFile file = await picker.PickSingleFileAsync();

       if (file != null)
       {
           using (IRandomAccessStream stream = await file.OpenAsync(FileAccessMode.Read))
           {
               // Create a PDF document
               PdfDocument document = new PdfDocument();
               PdfPage page = document.Pages.Add();
               PdfGraphics graphics = page.Graphics;

               // Load the image from the file
               using (var fileStream = stream.AsStreamForRead())
               {
                   PdfBitmap image = new PdfBitmap(fileStream);
                   graphics.DrawImage(image, 0, 0, page.GetClientSize().Width, page.GetClientSize().Height);
               }

               // Save the PDF document
               var savePicker = new FileSavePicker();
               savePicker.SuggestedStartLocation = PickerLocationId.Desktop;
               savePicker.FileTypeChoices.Add("PDF Document", new List<string>() { ".pdf" });
               savePicker.SuggestedFileName = "ConvertedPDF";
               StorageFile pdfFile = await savePicker.PickSaveFileAsync();

               if (pdfFile != null)
               {
                   using (var pdfStream = await pdfFile.OpenStreamForWriteAsync())
                   {
                       document.Save(pdfStream);
                   }
               }

               // Close the PDF document
               document.Close(true);
           }
       }
   }
   ```

### Word Document Conversion Example

1. **Install Open XML SDK**: You can add the Open XML SDK to your UWP project via NuGet.

   ```
   Install-Package DocumentFormat.OpenXml
   ```

2. **Code to Convert Image to Word Document**:

   ```csharp
   using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging;
   using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Wordprocessing;
   using Windows.Storage;
   using Windows.Storage.Pickers;
   using Windows.Storage.Streams;

   private async void ConvertImageToWord()
   {
       // Select an image file
       var picker = new FileOpenPicker();
       picker.ViewMode = PickerViewMode.Thumbnail;
       picker.SuggestedStartLocation = PickerLocationId.PicturesLibrary;
       picker.FileTypeFilter.Add(".jpg");
       picker.FileTypeFilter.Add(".png");
       StorageFile file = await picker.PickSingleFileAsync();

       if (file != null)
       {
           var savePicker = new FileSavePicker();
           savePicker.SuggestedStartLocation = PickerLocationId.Desktop;
           savePicker.FileTypeChoices.Add("Word Document", new List<string>() { ".docx" });
           savePicker.SuggestedFileName = "ConvertedDocument";
           StorageFile wordFile = await savePicker.PickSaveFileAsync();

           if (wordFile != null)
           {
               using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
               {
                   // Copy the image to MemoryStream
                   using (var stream = await file.OpenStreamForReadAsync())
                   {
                       await stream.CopyToAsync(ms);
                   }

                   using (WordprocessingDocument wordDocument = WordprocessingDocument.Create(wordFile.Path, DocumentFormat.OpenXml.WordprocessingDocumentType.Document))
                   {
                       // Add a main document part
                       var mainPart = wordDocument.AddMainDocumentPart();
                       mainPart.Document = new Document();
                       var body = new Body();
                       mainPart.Document.Append(body);

                       // Insert image
                       var imgPart = mainPart.AddImagePart(ImagePartType.Jpeg);
                       ms.Position = 0;
                       imgPart.FeedData(ms);

                       var extent = new Extent() { Cx = 990000L, Cy = 792000L }; // Width and height in EMUs.
                       var drawing = new Drawing(new DW.Inline(new DW.Extent() { Cx = extent.Cx, Cy = extent.Cy }));
                       body.Append(new Paragraph(new Run(drawing)));
                       mainPart.Document.Save();
                   }
               }
           }
       }
   }
   ```

### Conclusion

These examples demonstrate how to convert images to PDF and Word documents using libraries compatible with UWP. You can further refine and customize these implementations based on your requirements. Don't forget to handle exceptions and edge cases as needed for a production-ready application.

