llm_answer:
To allow a particular date field to be left blank (or to accept `null` values) in a Spring MVC application, you need to customize the way the `DateFormatter` handles empty strings. By default, `DateFormatter` throws an exception when it attempts to parse a blank string.

Here's how you can achieve this:

1. **Create a Custom Date Formatter**: Extend the `DateFormatter` to handle `null` or empty string more gracefully.

Here’s an example of how to create a custom date formatter:

```java
import org.springframework.format.datetime.DateFormatter;
import org.springframework.util.StringUtils;

import java.text.ParseException;
import java.util.Date;

public class NullableDateFormatter extends DateFormatter {

    public NullableDateFormatter(String pattern) {
        super(pattern);
    }

    @Override
    public Date parse(String text, Locale locale) throws ParseException {
        // Check for empty or null string
        if (StringUtils.isEmpty(text)) {
            return null; // return null instead of throwing an exception
        }
        return super.parse(text, locale); // fallback to the default parsing
    }
}
```

2. **Register Your Custom Formatter**: Update your Spring configuration to use this new formatter instead of the default `DateFormatter`.

Here’s how you can modify your XML configuration:

```xml
<beans:bean id="conversionService"
    class="org.springframework.format.support.FormattingConversionServiceFactoryBean">
    <beans:property name="converters">
        <beans:list>
            ...
        </beans:list>
    </beans:property>
    <beans:property name="formatters">
        <beans:set>
            <!-- Use the custom NullableDateFormatter -->
            <beans:bean id="nullableDateFormatter"
                class="com.yourpackage.NullableDateFormatter">
                <beans:constructor-arg>
                    <beans:value>dd/MM/yyyy</beans:value>
                </beans:constructor-arg>
            </beans:bean>
        </beans:set>
    </beans:property>
</beans:bean>
```

3. **Adjust Your Form Binding**: Ensure that the field in your form allows for empty inputs, e.g., in your JSP/HTML form, you should not enforce a required validation on the date field.

4. **Updating the Controller**: In your controller, make sure the date field of your command/object is of type `Date`. When the form is submitted with an empty date field, the `NullableDateFormatter` will allow it to be `null` without causing any exceptions.

### Example Controller Method

```java
@Controller
public class YourController {

    @RequestMapping(value = "/your-endpoint", method = RequestMethod.POST)
    public String handleSubmit(@ModelAttribute YourForm form) {
        Date dateField = form.getDateField(); // This will be null if the input was empty
        // Handle the dateField accordingly
        return "redirect:/somePage";
    }
}
```

With these changes, your application should now be able to accept null dates in Spring MVC whenever the field is left blank in a form.

