To ensure that your Navigation Drawer remains active and functional across different Activities or Fragments, you can utilize a Fragment-based architecture within a single Activity. This way, you will replace the content in the main part of your Activity with different Fragments, while keeping the Navigation Drawer intact. Here’s how to achieve that:

### Step-by-Step Implementation

1. **Use a Fragment for Every Navigation Option**: Instead of launching new Activities for different menu items, implement Fragments to display different content. For example, you might create `GPSOverviewFragment`, `TachoscheibeFragment`, etc.

2. **Fragment Transaction**: In your `onNavigationItemSelected` method, perform a Fragment transaction to replace the existing Fragment with the new one.

3. **Default Fragment Display**: Optionally, you can set a default Fragment to display when your Activity starts.

### Updated Code

Here's how you can modify your `MainActivity` to implement Fragment navigation using your Navigation Drawer:

```java
@Override
protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
    setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);
    Toolbar toolbar = (Toolbar) findViewById(R.id.toolbar);
    setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

    DrawerLayout drawer = (DrawerLayout) findViewById(R.id.drawer_layout);
    ActionBarDrawerToggle toggle = new ActionBarDrawerToggle(
            this, drawer, toolbar, R.string.navigation_drawer_open, R.string.navigation_drawer_close);
    drawer.addDrawerListener(toggle);
    toggle.syncState();

    NavigationView navigationView = (NavigationView) findViewById(R.id.nav_view);
    navigationView.setNavigationItemSelectedListener(this);

    // Load the default Fragment
    if (savedInstanceState == null) {
        loadFragment(new GPSOverviewFragment()); // Replace with your default Fragment
    }

    // Check for GPS provider...
}

// Implement the onNavigationItemSelected
@Override
public boolean onNavigationItemSelected(MenuItem item) {
    int id = item.getItemId();
    Fragment selectedFragment = null;

    if (id == R.id.gps_overview) {
        selectedFragment = new GPSOverviewFragment();
    } else if (id == R.id.tachoscheibe) {
        selectedFragment = new TachoscheibeFragment();
    } else if (id == R.id.settings) {
        selectedFragment = new SettingsFragment();
    } else if (id == R.id.exit) {
        finish();
        return true; // To prevent closing the drawer
    }

    if (selectedFragment != null) {
        loadFragment(selectedFragment);
    }

    DrawerLayout drawer = (DrawerLayout) findViewById(R.id.drawer_layout);
    drawer.closeDrawer(GravityCompat.START);
    return true;
}

// Method to perform the Fragment transaction
private void loadFragment(Fragment fragment) {
    // Replace the current Fragment with the selected one
    FragmentTransaction transaction = getSupportFragmentManager().beginTransaction();
    transaction.replace(R.id.container, fragment);
    transaction.commit();
}
```

### Fragment Class Example

Here's a basic example of how you might define a Fragment:

```java
