To center the `ActionBarDrawerToggle` within the toolbar on your Android application, you need to consider both the layout parameters of the toolbar and the `DrawerToggle` itself. You have two potential approaches as you’ve identified: adjusting the toolbar height to default and centering the toggle icon. 

Here’s how to address both cases.

### Solution 1: Use Default Toolbar Height

If you are willing to use the default toolbar height, you just need to remove any layout parameters that force your toolbar height to be variable or non-standard. Since Android's default action bar height varies across different devices, it might be challenging to keep it consistent manually. 

```java
// Remove any hardcoded height or wrap content setting.
// Just make sure the toolbar is using the full width and default height.
LinearLayout.LayoutParams lp = new LinearLayout.LayoutParams(
        LayoutParams.MATCH_PARENT, LayoutParams.WRAP_CONTENT);
tool.setLayoutParams(lp);
```

By keeping the height at `WRAP_CONTENT`, Android will automatically take care of the height based on the content and styling, effectively centering your toggle icon without doing anything manually.

### Solution 2: Center the Toggle Icon Manually

If you want to manually control the appearance of the `DrawerToggle`, you can modify the `Toolbar` and `ImageView` you are using to make it look like your toggle icon is centered.

Here's how you can approach this with a custom layout:

1. **Create a Custom Layout for Toolbar**: Instead of using the default toolbar provided by Android, you can create a custom layout that has an `ImageView` for the toggle icon, which you can position in the center. You can also include additional `Views` if you need.

```xml
// res/layout/custom_toolbar.xml
<LinearLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content">

    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/toggle_icon"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="center"
        android:src="@drawable/ic_toggle_icon" />  <!-- Your toggle icon drawable -->

</LinearLayout>
```

2. **Inflate your Custom Layout in Java**:

You then need to inflate this layout in your activity code and use it in place of the default toolbar.

```java
protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);

    mDrawerLayout = new DrawerLayout(getApplicationContext());
    // ... [initialize your layout and components]

    // Inflate your custom toolbar layout
    LayoutInflater inflater = (LayoutInflater) getSystemService(Context.LAYOUT_INFLATER_SERVICE);
    View customToolbar = inflater.inflate(R.layout.custom_toolbar, null);

    // Find the ImageView that will act as the toggle icon
    ImageView toggleIcon = customToolbar.findViewById(R.id.toggle_icon);

    // Set the click listener for toggle
    toggleIcon.setOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
        @Override
