I'm glad to hear that you've made progress in binding UI-Grid with Firebase! This is a common challenge when working with Angular, especially considering Firebase's asynchronous data handling. Let's refine your solution and clarify the handling of promises between the UI-Grid's cell editing and Firebase saving mechanism.

### Updated Solution

To achieve proper 3-way data binding between UI-Grid and Firebase while ensuring that your changes persist upon editing, you can follow the outlined approach below:

#### HTML View
You already have your grid setup correctly:
```html
<!-- UI-Grid -->
<div ui-grid="gridOptions" class="ui-grid" ui-grid-resize-columns ui-grid-edit ui-grid-row-edit ui-grid-cellNav></div>
```

#### Controller View
Here’s a complete example based on your findings while fine-tuning the promise management:

```javascript
var ref = new Firebase("https://commentme.firebaseio.com/comments");    

// Create an AngularFire reference to the data
var sync = $firebase(ref);

// Create a synchronized array for use in our HTML code
$scope.comments = sync.$asArray();

$scope.newComment = { text: '', date: '' };

// Add Comment Function
$scope.addComment = function() {
    $scope.comments.$add($scope.newComment);           
    $scope.newComment = { text: '', date: '' };
};

// UI Grid Options  
$scope.gridOptions = {
    enableSorting: true,
    columnDefs: [
        { name: 'text', field: 'text', editableCellTemplate: '<input type="text" ng-model="COL_FIELD"/>' },
        { name: 'date', field: 'date', editableCellTemplate: '<input type="text" ng-model="COL_FIELD"/>' }
    ],
    data: $scope.comments,
    onRegisterApi: function(gridApi) {
        // Set gridApi on scope
        $scope.gridApi = gridApi;                  
        gridApi.rowEdit.on.saveRow($scope, $scope.saveRow);
    }
};

// Save Row Function
$scope.saveRow = function(rowEntity) {
    var promise = $scope.comments.$save(rowEntity); // Save to Firebase

    promise.then(function(ref) {
        // Handling success
        console.log("Saved successfully:", ref.key());
        // In case you want to do something after save
        // e.g. notify user of success
    }, function(error) {
        // Handling error
        console.error("Error:", error);
        // In case you want to notify user of error
    });

    // Create a fake promise - UI-Grid needs a promise to allow for editing
    var deferred = $q.defer();
    $scope.gridApi.rowEdit.setSavePromise($scope.gridApi.grid, rowEntity, deferred.promise);

    // Handle the promise from Firebase
    promise.then(function() {
        deferred.resolve(); // Resolve when the Firebase save is successful
    }).catch(function() {
        deferred.reject(); // Reject in case there was an error saving
    });
};
```

### Key Points in the Implementation:

1. **Editable Template**: You are using `editableCellTemplate` correctly. Here, we directly bind `ng-model` to `COL_FIELD`, which represents the value for that cell.

2. **Promise Management**:
    - Instead of creating a fake promise, use the promise returned by `$save()`. This ensures that UI-Grid properly handles the edit state based on the actual completion of the save operation.
    - Call `deferred.resolve()` after the successful save, and `deferred.reject()` if there is an error.

3. **Error Handling**: It's always good to implement error handling to inform the user of success or failure when saving data.

This allows you to achieve 3-way binding between your UI-Grid and Firebase, where any changes to the grid cells will persist back to Firebase seamlessly. With this pattern, you can effectively manage the asynchronous nature of data operations while maintaining a responsive UI.

