To resolve the issue of converting a selected entry from your `AutoCompleteTextView` to the desired `User` property (e.g., the `name` property), you can override the `toString()` method of the `ArrayAdapter` that you are using. However, you might also want to customize how the selected item is displayed in the `AutoCompleteTextView` without modifying the original `User` class's `toString()` method.

### Here’s how you can achieve that:

1. **Create a Custom ArrayAdapter:**
   Subclass `ArrayAdapter<User>` and override the `getItem()` and/or `getDropDownView()` methods to control how the items are represented.

2. **Customize `getAutoCompleteText()` Method:**
   Override the `getFilter()` method to filter your users correctly based on the input.

3. **Set the selected item in `AutoCompleteTextView`:**
   Update the `AutoCompleteTextView` to display the correct property when an item is selected.

Here's a complete example implementation:

```java
import android.content.Context;
import android.widget.ArrayAdapter;
import android.widget.AutoCompleteTextView;
import android.widget.Filter;
import android.widget.Filterable;

import androidx.annotation.NonNull;
import androidx.annotation.Nullable;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class UserAdapter extends ArrayAdapter<User> implements Filterable {

    private List<User> originalUsers;  // Original list of users
    private List<User> filteredUsers;   // List of filtered users

    public UserAdapter(@NonNull Context context, List<User> users) {
        super(context, android.R.layout.simple_dropdown_item_1line, users);
        this.originalUsers = new ArrayList<>(users);
        this.filteredUsers = new ArrayList<>(users);
    }

    @NonNull
    @Override
    public Filter getFilter() {
        return new Filter() {
            @Override
            protected FilterResults performFiltering(CharSequence constraint) {
                FilterResults results = new FilterResults();
                List<User> suggestions = new ArrayList<>();

                if (constraint != null) {
                    String filterPattern = constraint.toString().toLowerCase().trim();

                    for (User user : originalUsers) {
                        if (user.getName().toLowerCase().contains(filterPattern)) {
                            suggestions.add(user);
                        }
                    }
                }

                results.values = suggestions;
                results.count = suggestions.size();
                return results;
            }

            @Override
            protected void publishResults(CharSequence constraint, FilterResults results) {
                filteredUsers.clear();
                filteredUsers.addAll((List<User>) results.values);
                notifyDataSetChanged();
            }
        };
    }

    @NonNull
    @Override
    public User getItem(int position) {
        return filteredUsers.get(position);
    }

    @Override
    public int getCount() {
        return filteredUsers.size();
    }

    @Override
    public String getDropDownView(int position, @Nullable View convertView, @NonNull ViewGroup parent) {
        User user = getItem(position);
        // Here you can return the formatted string for the dropdown items
        return user.getName(); // Set how dropdown will be displayed
    }
}

// Usage in Activity/Fragment
AutoCompleteTextView autoCompleteTextView = findViewById(R.id.autoCompleteTextView);
UserAdapter userAdapter = new UserAdapter(this, userList); // userList is the list of User objects.
autoCompleteTextView.setAdapter(userAdapter);

// Set a listener to obtain the selected User's name when an item is selected
autoCompleteTextView.setOnItemClickListener((parent, view, position, id) -> {
    User selectedUser = (User) parent.getItemAtPosition(position);
    autoCompleteTextView.setText(selectedUser.getName()); // Set the name of the user to the TextView
});
